{"id":1229,"date":"2020-11-24T12:15:14","date_gmt":"2020-11-24T20:15:14","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1229"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:28:13","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:28:13","slug":"codrone-arduino-arrays","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-arrays\/","title":{"rendered":"Arrays"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96c58\" name=\"6a2215db96c58\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are arrays?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Variables are a great way to store one value that you can use later on in your program. But what if you need to store multiple values at the same time, like keeping score in a multi-player video game? This is where arrays are useful. One array can hold as many values as you decide to set!<\/p>\n<p>One of the best ways to think of arrays is like a row of boxes. Each box, which stores a value, is called an element. Keep in mind that computers start counting at 0, so boxes 0-7, like we have below, are actually called myArray[8] when they&#8217;re together.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1230&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]One more important thing to remember: the data that&#8217;s in an array has to be the same type. You cannot have an array with values like [apple, 78, J, CoDrone], but you can have an array with values like [78, -56, 324, 760452].[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96d94\" name=\"6a2215db96d94\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Storing values<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you&#8217;re lazy, you&#8217;ll love arrays since you don&#8217;t need to name every single element! You can just group all of the elements under one title (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">myArray<\/code>) and number them from 0 to whatever your last element is.<\/p>\n<p>In this example, we wanted to store 8 different integers: 1, 7, 15, -23, -92, 0, 5, and 57. All of them would start out with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">myArray[]<\/code> and have their element number next to it. If we wanted to recall a specific element&#8217;s value later on in the program, we would just need to use the element&#8217;s number. For example, if we wanted to recall the value 1, we could write <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">myArray[0]<\/code>, since 1 is represented by element 0.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1231&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96e29\" name=\"6a2215db96e29\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using arrays<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just like variables, arrays need to be initialized at the beginning of your program before they can be used. Most arrays follow this format:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">data type ArrayName[Array Size]<\/pre>\n<p>What all of that means:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Data type is the kind of data that you&#8217;re storing in the array. If you can&#8217;t remember the data types, check the Variables lesson. All of the elements in the array have to be the same data type.<\/li>\n<li>ArrayName is the name of your array. You can name it whenever you want!<\/li>\n<li>Array Size is the number of elements that will be in your array. Don&#8217;t forget that computers start counting at 0, not 1!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Some important things to keep in mind:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you store or read past an array\u2019s initialized size, you won\u2019t receive an error. It\u00a0will just ruin your program without warning!<\/li>\n<li>Elements within an array act just like variables<em>.\u00a0<\/em>They can be overwritten and called within the program whenever you want.<\/li>\n<li>Just so you don&#8217;t forget because this is really important: computers start counting at 0. Your first element will be <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">ArrayName[0]<\/code>!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96eab\" name=\"6a2215db96eab\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Array example<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Test this code out, and make sure your serial monitor is open!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int newArray[5] = {10, 15, 22, 9, -5}; \r\n\r\nvoid setup() { \r\n    Serial.begin(9600);\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() {  \r\n    for ( int i = 0; i &lt;= 4 ; i++ ) { \r\n        Serial.println(newArray[i]); \r\n    }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>The pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initialize <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">newArray[5]<\/code> with values of 10, 15, 22, 9, and -5.<\/li>\n<li>Begin communication at a baud rate of 9600.<\/li>\n<li>Set i&#8217;s value as 0, and as long as i is less than or equal to 4, increase i by 1.<\/li>\n<li>if i is between 0 and 4, print i&#8217;s value in the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Your serial monitor should look like the image below.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1232&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96f44\" name=\"6a2215db96f44\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Arrays with buzzers<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Arrays can be used for anything, but for this lesson you&#8217;re going to use arrays to make some music on your CoDrone&#8217;s remote control!<\/p>\n<p>The first thing you need to do in Arduino is set up the CoDrone library. Next, you need to make an array (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">melody<\/code>) and set your notes up inside. Quick tip about music: a set of notes are in a scale, and scales go up or down in pitch. Usually, there are eight notes in one scale. For this program, you&#8217;re only using eight notes.<\/p>\n<p>You need to make one more array for how long your notes will be, or the duration. Our array is called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">noteDurations<\/code>. The 0th element, 1, is about 1 second long, the 3rd element in this array, 4, is about 0.25 seconds long, and the 7th element, 8, is about 0.125 seconds long.<\/p>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror notranslate cm-s-default\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-hscrollbar\">\n<div>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include&lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nint melody[] = {\r\n  NOTE_C3, NOTE_D3, NOTE_E3, NOTE_F3, NOTE_G3, NOTE_A3, NOTE_B3, NOTE_C4\r\n};\r\n\r\nint noteDurations[] = {\r\n  1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\r\n};<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-scroll\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-sizer\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-lines\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-measure\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-linenumber CodeMirror-gutter-elt\">\n<div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-measure\"><\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-cursors\">\n<div class=\"CodeMirror-cursor\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db96ffd\" name=\"6a2215db96ffd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Arrays and for loops: Part 1<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You can use a for loop to access your array and have your buzzers play a scale! In void setup(), write a for loop that starts a variable (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">thisNote<\/code>) at 0, and when the variable is less than or equal to 7, a value of 1 is added. As long as the variable&#8217;s value is between 0 and 7, the corresponding note will play for 0.25 seconds.<\/p>\n<p>We added a 500-millisecond delay so there&#8217;s enough space in between this scale and what&#8217;s coming next, but you don&#8217;t absolutely have to add this.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup() {\r\nfor (int thisNote = 0; thisNote&lt;=7; thisNote++){\r\n  CoDrone.Buzz(melody[thisNote], noteDurations[3]);\r\n}\r\ndelay(500);<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db9706d\" name=\"6a2215db9706d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Arrays and for loops: Part 2<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Use another for loop to play your scale, but this time, write it so that your variable starts at 7. When the variable is greater than or equal to 0, a value of 1 is subtracted. As long as the variable&#8217;s value is between 7 and 0, the corresponding note will play for 0.25 seconds.<\/p>\n<p>Again, we added a delay of 500 milliseconds, but you still don&#8217;t absolutely have to do this.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">for (int thisNote = 7; thisNote&gt;=0; thisNote--){\r\n  CoDrone.Buzz(melody[thisNote], noteDurations[3]);\r\n}\r\ndelay(500);<\/pre>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\"><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db970e1\" name=\"6a2215db970e1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Play a song<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]As long as the elements in your array are notes, you can access them to play songs! To start, make sure each commands starts with the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.Buzz<\/code> function.<\/p>\n<p>Since you have two arrays &#8212; one for the notes and one for the duration &#8212; you need to use them both between one set of parentheses and separate them with a comma. The first element should be the note and the second element should be the duration.<\/p>\n<p>If you have this in your code:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[2]);\r\ndelay(50);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[2]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[1],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[3],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[2],noteDurations[0]);<\/pre>\n<p>It will look like this without the array:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_C3,3);\r\ndelay(50);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_C3,3);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_D3,2);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_C3,2);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_F3,2);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(NOTE_E3,1);<\/pre>\n<p>Wondering about the 50-millisecond delay? It&#8217;s in between two of the same notes that are next to each other. Without that delay, the computer would play both notes so fast it would just sound like one note to you!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db97145\" name=\"6a2215db97145\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This is what your final code should look like:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include&lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nint melody[] = {\r\n  NOTE_C3, NOTE_D3, NOTE_E3, NOTE_F3, NOTE_G3, NOTE_A3, NOTE_B3, NOTE_C4\r\n};\r\n\r\nint noteDurations[] = {\r\n  1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\r\n};\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\nfor (int thisNote = 0; thisNote&lt;=7; thisNote++){\r\n CoDrone.Buzz(melody[thisNote], noteDurations[3]);\r\n}\r\ndelay(500);\r\n\r\nfor (int thisNote = 7; thisNote&gt;=0; thisNote--){\r\n  CoDrone.Buzz(melody[thisNote], noteDurations[3]);\r\n}\r\ndelay(500);\r\n\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[2]);\r\ndelay(50);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[2]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[1],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[0],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[3],noteDurations[1]);\r\nCoDrone.Buzz(melody[2],noteDurations[0]);\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop(){\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Recognize the song that just played?<\/p>\n<p>Note: all of this is in void setup. If you want any part of this to loop, you can put that portion of your code in void loop. Just make sure your arrays are at the top of your program, right underneath the CoDrone library!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215db971a3\" name=\"6a2215db971a3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Make Some Noise!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Use notes in an array to have your buzzers play a song!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your program must use at least one array that has notes in it.<\/li>\n<li>Your song should be at least 5 seconds long.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Bonus: if you can read music, use that to help you program your favorite song![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 What are arrays? [vc_column_text]Variables are a great way to store one value that you can use later on in your program. But what if you need to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-arrays\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1749,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1812,1826,1482,853,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2163,2205,2164,2165,2137,2122],"class_list":["post-1229","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-arrays","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-lists","lesson-tag-programming","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1229","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":3,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1229\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2479,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1229\/revisions\/2479"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1749"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1229"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1229"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1229"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}